## Type Safety with Generics

```java
// Without generics: ClassCastException at runtime
List list = new ArrayList();
list.add("hello");
list.add(42);  // No error at compile time!
String s = (String) list.get(1);  // ClassCastException at runtime!

// With generics: caught at compile time
List<String> strings = new ArrayList<>();
strings.add("hello");
// strings.add(42);  // COMPILE ERROR: int cannot be added to List<String>
String s2 = strings.get(0);  // No cast needed — type is known
```

## Type Erasure

```java
// These are THE SAME at runtime:
List<String> stringList = new ArrayList<>();
List<Integer> intList = new ArrayList<>();
System.out.println(stringList.getClass() == intList.getClass());  // true!
System.out.println(stringList.getClass().getName());  // java.util.ArrayList

// Cannot do at runtime:
List<String> ls = new ArrayList<>();
if (ls instanceof List<String>) { }  // COMPILE ERROR — erased
String[] arr = new String[10];
// T[] arr = new T[10];  // COMPILE ERROR — T erased

// Workaround with Class<T>:
public class TypedList<T> {
    private final Class<T> type;
    private final List<T> items = new ArrayList<>();

    public TypedList(Class<T> type) { this.type = type; }

    public boolean isType(Object obj) {
        return type.isInstance(obj);  // Works at runtime — Class retains type
    }

    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public T cast(Object obj) {
        return type.cast(obj);  // Checked cast — throws ClassCastException if wrong
    }
}
```

## Bounded Type Parameters

```java
// Upper bound: T must be Comparable
public static <T extends Comparable<T>> T max(List<T> list) {
    return list.stream()
        .max(Comparator.naturalOrder())
        .orElseThrow(IllegalArgumentException::new);
}
// max(List.of(3, 1, 4, 1, 5)) = 5
// max(List.of("apple", "banana")) = "banana"

// Multiple bounds: T must extend Serializable AND implement Comparable
public <T extends Serializable & Comparable<T>> void processAndSort(List<T> items) { ... }

// Recursive bound: used for self-referential types
public class Builder<SELF extends Builder<SELF>> {
    protected SELF self() { return (SELF) this; }
    public SELF withName(String name) { this.name = name; return self(); }
}

public class OrderBuilder extends Builder<OrderBuilder> {
    public OrderBuilder withTotal(BigDecimal total) {
        this.total = total; return self();
    }
}
Order order = new OrderBuilder().withName("Test").withTotal(BigDecimal.TEN).build();
// Method chain returns OrderBuilder — not just Builder
```

## Generic Method vs Generic Class

```java
// Generic class: type parameter per instance
public class Pair<A, B> {
    private final A first;
    private final B second;
    public Pair(A first, B second) { this.first = first; this.second = second; }
    public A getFirst() { return first; }
    public B getSecond() { return second; }
}
Pair<String, Integer> pair = new Pair<>("hello", 42);

// Generic method: type parameter per call
public static <T> List<T> repeat(T value, int times) {
    List<T> result = new ArrayList<>();
    for (int i = 0; i < times; i++) result.add(value);
    return result;
}
List<String> words = repeat("Java", 5);  // Type inferred by compiler
List<Integer> numbers = repeat(0, 10);
```

## Production Reality

Generics are ubiquitous in Spring: `ResponseEntity<T>`, `Optional<T>`, `Page<T>`, `CompletableFuture<T>`. Understanding type erasure matters when working with frameworks that use reflection — Jackson's TypeReference, Spring's `ParameterizedTypeReference` for WebClient responses. Use `TypeToken` (Guava) or `ParameterizedTypeReference` when you need to retain generic type information at runtime.

## Why List<String> is not a subtype of List<Object>

```java
// Covariance problem:
List<String> strings = new ArrayList<>();
List<Object> objects = strings;  // COMPILE ERROR — and rightly so!
// If this were allowed:
objects.add(42);  // Adding Integer to what is actually List<String>!
String s = strings.get(0);  // ClassCastException!

// Arrays ARE covariant (legacy Java — broken!)
String[] strArr = new String[3];
Object[] objArr = strArr;  // Allowed — but dangerous
objArr[0] = 42;  // ArrayStoreException at runtime!
```

## Wildcards with PECS

```java
// ? extends T — Producer (read from it)
public double sumNumbers(List<? extends Number> numbers) {
    double sum = 0;
    for (Number n : numbers) {  // Read as Number — safe
        sum += n.doubleValue();
    }
    // numbers.add(1.5);  // COMPILE ERROR — can't add (don't know exact type)
    return sum;
}
// Can call with: List<Integer>, List<Double>, List<Number>
double total = sumNumbers(List.of(1, 2, 3));     // List<Integer>
double total2 = sumNumbers(List.of(1.5, 2.5));   // List<Double>

// ? super T — Consumer (write to it)
public void addIntegers(List<? super Integer> dest, int count) {
    for (int i = 1; i <= count; i++) {
        dest.add(i);  // Can add Integer — safe (dest accepts Integer and supertypes)
    }
    // Integer first = dest.get(0);  // COMPILE ERROR — could be List<Number>, can't assume Integer
    Object obj = dest.get(0);  // Only safe as Object
}
// Can call with: List<Integer>, List<Number>, List<Object>
addIntegers(new ArrayList<Integer>(), 5);  // OK
addIntegers(new ArrayList<Number>(), 5);   // OK
addIntegers(new ArrayList<Object>(), 5);   // OK

// PECS in action — Collections.copy:
// void copy(List<? super T> dest, List<? extends T> src)
// dest is a consumer (super T) — things are written to it
// src is a producer (extends T) — things are read from it
List<Number> dest = new ArrayList<>(List.of(0.0, 0.0, 0.0));
List<Integer> src = List.of(1, 2, 3);
Collections.copy(dest, src);  // Works — copies Integers into Number list
```

## Generic Method vs Wildcard

```java
// Equivalent: use generic method when return type or multiple params are related
public static <T> void swap(List<T> list, int i, int j) {
    T temp = list.get(i);
    list.set(i, list.get(j));
    list.set(j, temp);
    // T needed here because temp must be the same type as what we get/set
}

// Wildcard: use when no relationship between types is needed
public static void printList(List<?> list) {
    list.forEach(System.out::println);  // Just printing — don't care about type
}
// List<?> is simpler than List<T> when T is not needed

// Helper with wildcard to generic:
public void someMethod(List<?> list) {
    swapFirst(list);  // Wildcard — can't do swap internally
}

private <T> void swapFirst(List<T> list) {
    // Can do typed operations here
    if (list.size() > 1) {
        T first = list.get(0);
        list.set(0, list.get(1));
        list.set(1, first);
    }
}
```

## Production Reality

PECS is most relevant when writing utility/library code or service interfaces that need flexibility. In application code, you'll often use concrete generic types (`List<Order>`, `Map<String, User>`) rather than wildcards. Wildcards appear most in: utility methods (like the ones in `Collections`), Spring's `ResponseEntity<? extends BaseDTO>`, and codec/mapper configurations that work with type hierarchies. The main rule to remember: if clients of your API will call with various subtype collections, use `? extends T` in the parameter.

## Generic Base Service

```java
// Base service — write once, use for any entity
public abstract class BaseService<T, ID, R extends JpaRepository<T, ID>> {

    @Autowired
    protected R repository;

    public Optional<T> findById(ID id) {
        return repository.findById(id);
    }

    public T findByIdOrThrow(ID id) {
        return repository.findById(id)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new EntityNotFoundException(
                getEntityName() + " not found: " + id));
    }

    public List<T> findAll() {
        return repository.findAll();
    }

    public T save(T entity) {
        return repository.save(entity);
    }

    public void deleteById(ID id) {
        if (!repository.existsById(id)) {
            throw new EntityNotFoundException(getEntityName() + " not found: " + id);
        }
        repository.deleteById(id);
    }

    protected abstract String getEntityName();
}

// Concrete service — only the specific methods:
@Service
public class OrderService extends BaseService<Order, Long, OrderRepository> {
    @Override
    protected String getEntityName() { return "Order"; }

    // Order-specific methods:
    public List<Order> findByUser(Long userId) {
        return repository.findByUserId(userId);
    }

    @Transactional
    public Order createOrder(CreateOrderRequest req) {
        Order order = new Order(req);
        return save(order);  // From BaseService
    }
}
```

## Type-Safe Heterogeneous Container

```java
// Map typed by Class — get any type with compile-time safety
public class TypeSafeRegistry {
    private final Map<Class<?>, Object> instances = new HashMap<>();

    public <T> void register(Class<T> type, T instance) {
        instances.put(type, instance);
    }

    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public <T> Optional<T> get(Class<T> type) {
        return Optional.ofNullable(type.cast(instances.get(type)));
        // type.cast() is a checked cast — won't return wrong type
    }
}

// Usage:
TypeSafeRegistry registry = new TypeSafeRegistry();
registry.register(UserService.class, userService);
registry.register(OrderService.class, orderService);

UserService us = registry.get(UserService.class).orElseThrow();
// No cast needed — type is preserved by Class<T> key
```

## Generic Result Type

```java
// Result<T, E>: success with value T or failure with error E
public sealed interface Result<T, E> permits Success, Failure {

    record Success<T, E>(T value) implements Result<T, E> {}
    record Failure<T, E>(E error) implements Result<T, E> {}

    static <T, E> Result<T, E> success(T value) { return new Success<>(value); }
    static <T, E> Result<T, E> failure(E error) { return new Failure<>(error); }

    default boolean isSuccess() { return this instanceof Success; }

    default <R> Result<R, E> map(Function<T, R> mapper) {
        return switch (this) {
            case Success<T, E>(var v) -> Result.success(mapper.apply(v));
            case Failure<T, E> f -> (Result<R, E>) f;
        };
    }

    default T orElseThrow(Function<E, RuntimeException> errorMapper) {
        return switch (this) {
            case Success<T, E>(var v) -> v;
            case Failure<T, E>(var e) -> throw errorMapper.apply(e);
        };
    }
}

// Usage:
public Result<Order, String> createOrder(CreateOrderRequest req) {
    if (req.getItems().isEmpty()) {
        return Result.failure("Order must have at least one item");
    }
    Order order = orderRepo.save(new Order(req));
    return Result.success(order);
}

// Calling code:
Result<Order, String> result = orderService.createOrder(req);
Order order = result.orElseThrow(error -> new InvalidOrderException(error));
// Or:
switch (result) {
    case Success(var order2) -> log.info("Created: {}", order2.getId());
    case Failure(var error) -> log.warn("Failed: {}", error);
}
```

## Production Reality

Generic base classes reduce boilerplate significantly in CRUD-heavy applications. However, be careful about making the base too generic — if it becomes a god class that every team touches, it becomes a merge conflict nightmare. Keep base classes minimal: provide shared CRUD operations, let concrete classes add business methods. For the generic `Result` type, Spring's `ResponseEntity<T>` already serves this purpose at the HTTP layer — consider if you really need another layer of wrapping.
